The Numbers — “Four Brings a Plus-One”

The numbers are the cast. Parentheses decide who gets stuck together. An operation gives them something to do.

2 wants to keep the crew small. 3 wants a solo. 4 brings four of everything, including opinions. i can turn anybody a quarter-turn counterclockwise. -i turns them the other way.

Tonight’s episode is (2+3i)(4-i).

⸻

INT. THE COMPLEX PLANE — BACKSTAGE

Two performers stand in the aisle. Three more wait on the balcony.

2: That’s everyone. We can fit in one cab.

3, from the balcony: Why am I up here?

i: You said you wanted to go up in the world.

3: I meant professionally.

4 bursts through the stage door. Three identical groups follow him in.

2: What did you do?

4: I made four copies of the act. We’ve got eight in the aisle and twelve on the balcony.

3: Did you make four copies of my solo?

4: Yes.

3: What are you even calling it now?

4: A dispute.

A stagehand in a backwards headset wheels in the original five performers on a platform.

2: Who’s this?

4: My minus-one.

-i: Give me the original act. I turn it clockwise.

The platform swings. Three balcony performers roll down into the aisle. The two from the aisle descend through a trapdoor.

2, leaning over the hole: Are you all right?

VOICE FROM BELOW: We’re fine. There’s a bar.

Two of the twelve balcony performers immediately volunteer to go down and get them. They pass the pair coming up in the elevator. All four miss the show.

STAGE MANAGER: Eleven in the aisle. Ten on the balcony. Places!

3: I still get my solo, right?

From the balcony, four Threes shout “YES” at once.

Cut to credits.

⸻

The stunt is the calculation. 4 makes four copies of the original cast, giving 8+12i. -i turns one original copy clockwise, sending its three imaginary units onto the positive real axis and its two real units down the imaginary axis. That gives 3-2i. Put the crews together and you get 11+10i.

And the same cast can go on an adventure. Give i a door that rotates the whole party every time they pass through it. On the third pass, 2 is convinced they’re lost. 3 notices the same scratch on the wall, one quarter-turn closer each time. The audience can figure out i^4=1 a beat before the characters do. Then they come through the fourth door and find 4 waiting with four cabs.